# log_report.py

A small Python script that reads app log files and turns them into a CSV report + JSON summary. I wrote this mostly to practice string handling, pathlib, and reading/writing files safely (without the whole thing crashing the moment it hits one bad line or a missing file).

## What it actually does

You give it one or more log files that look like this:

```
2026-09-10 08:12:03 INFO  Server started on port 8080
2026-09-10 08:14:51 WARNING Disk usage above 80%
2026-09-10 08:15:02 ERROR Failed to connect to database
```

and it spits out:

- `report.csv` – every parsed log line as a row
- `summary.json` – counts by level, counts by hour, a handful of sample error messages
- `problems.log` – anything that went wrong along the way (missing file, empty file, bad encoding, lines that didn't match the format)

Nothing fancy, just a straightforward parse -> summarize -> write pipeline.

## Running it

There's no setup needed, no dependencies outside the standard library.

```
python log_report.py
```

Running it directly will generate a sample log folder (`demo_logs/`) with a mix of good lines, a blank line, a malformed line, and even a file that doesn't exist on purpose — just so you can see how the error handling behaves. Output goes into `demo_output/`.

If you want to point it at your own logs, import the functions instead of running the file directly:

```python
from log_report import run

summary = run(["path/to/app.log", "path/to/other.log"], output_dir="reports")
```

## Why it's built this way

A few small decisions worth knowing about if you're reading the code:

- Bad lines/files don't raise exceptions — they get collected into a `problems` list and reported at the end. The idea is one bad file shouldn't kill a batch job that's processing twenty others.
- Encoding is always explicit (`utf-8`), never left to whatever the OS defaults to. Saves you from the classic "works on my machine" bug.
- Uses `pathlib` everywhere instead of raw string paths, so it behaves the same on Windows/Mac/Linux.
- Output directories get created automatically if they don't exist yet.

## Files in here

- `log_report.py` — the whole thing, one file
- `demo_logs/`, `demo_output/` — generated when you run the script, safe to delete and regenerate
- `report.csv` — sample output already included so you can peek at the format without running anything

That's about it. Feel free to poke around the code, it's commented pretty heavily since I built it partly as a learning exercise.
